<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yumten</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Yumtän</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yumten was one of two claimants to the Tibetan throne after the death of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langdarma">Langdarma</a> around 842, the other being his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osung">Ösung</a>. Backed by a rival faction of the nobility, he is said to have held power in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> while Ösung's line held other districts. Their rival successions marked the collapse of the Tibetan Empire and the beginning of the Era of Fragmentation.</p></div>
